# PlantOps — installation configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` beside `docker-compose.yml`, fill in the six values
# under REQUIRED, and run `./bootstrap.sh`.
#
#   cp .env.template .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#   ./bootstrap.sh
#
# ## Why this file is long
#
# `libs/config` validates the whole environment at boot and *refuses to start*
# on a bad value, listing every problem at once. That is the right behaviour —
# a misconfigured IAM should not serve one request — but on a machine we cannot
# reach it turns into a support call unless the meaning of each variable is
# written down where the operator is already looking. So every variable the
# application validates appears below, including the ones the stack sets for
# you: those are commented out, with what they will be and why.
#
# ## Rules
#
#   * `chmod 600` this file. It holds the signing key and two database
#     passwords.
#   * Do not quote values unless they contain spaces. `KEY=value`, no export.
#   * A blank value is not the same as an absent one. Leave a line commented
#     out rather than assigning it nothing.
#   * Changing anything here takes effect on the next `docker compose up -d`.
#     Changing a *database* value after installation does not migrate the data
#     that is already there.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REQUIRED — the install will not proceed without these
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Which release this is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The tag every PlantOps image in the bundle carries. Pre-filled by the bundle
# you were given; change it only when upgrading, and only to a version you have
# loaded (`docker load -i images/plantops-<version>.tar`).
PLANTOPS_VERSION=REPLACED_BY_BUILD_BUNDLE

# ── Database passwords ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two roles, and the split is the most consequential thing in this file.
#
# The OWNER owns every table and runs migrations. The APP role owns nothing and
# serves every request, which is what makes the row-level security policies
# apply to it: in PostgreSQL a table's owner is exempt from its own policies,
# independently of SUPERUSER. An install that serves traffic as the owner has
# silently switched off every tenant boundary in the system while looking
# perfectly healthy — so `bootstrap.sh` creates both roles and the application
# refuses to start if it detects it is running as the owner.
#
# Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
PLANTOPS_APP_PASSWORD=

# ── Token signing key ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RS256, 2048 bits or more. Every access token this deployment issues is signed
# with it, and every module that trusts this IAM verifies against its public
# half. Generate a pair on the server, not on a laptop:
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out jwt.key
#   openssl rsa -in jwt.key -pubout -out jwt.pub
#
# Paste each on ONE line with the newlines written as \n — the application
# unescapes them. `JWT_SIGNING_KEY_ID` is any short stable name; it appears in
# every token's header and is what makes rotation possible later.
JWT_SIGNING_KEY_ID=
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── The first platform credential ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Minimum 32 characters. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# Used EXACTLY ONCE, by the migration that seeds the platform identity, and
# then by `bootstrap.sh` to create your organisation and first administrator.
# After that it is dead weight and a live credential at the same time, which is
# the worst combination — so the last thing bootstrap prints is the command to
# rotate it, and you should run it before you close the terminal.
#
# Once rotated, delete this line. The application never reads it: it starts
# perfectly well without it, and a finished installation should not have it.
PLATFORM_BOOTSTRAP_SECRET=

# ── Your organisation and its first administrator ──────────────────────────
# `bootstrap.sh` creates these through the API, the same way the platform
# console would, so nothing here is a special case: a client, a root scope node,
# a system role carrying the tenant-administration permissions, and a user bound
# to the role at the root.
#
# The slug is what the login screen asks for alongside the email. Lowercase
# letters, digits and hyphens.
#
# The password must be at least 12 characters. Change it after the first login;
# it is written in this file, and this file is on disk.
PLANTOPS_CLIENT_NAME=
PLANTOPS_CLIENT_SLUG=
PLANTOPS_ADMIN_EMAIL=
PLANTOPS_ADMIN_NAME=
PLANTOPS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# OPTIONAL — sensible defaults, change with a reason
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Where the stack listens ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one port this installation publishes. Everything else — the API, the
# console, Postgres, Redis — is reachable only on the stack's private network.
#
# Terminate TLS in front of this port with your own certificate. If you do, set
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS below to the network your terminator sits on, or every
# unauthenticated caller will share one rate-limit bucket.
PLANTOPS_HTTP_PORT=8080

# Comma-separated CIDRs of proxies in front of this stack whose
# `X-Forwarded-For` may be believed. Empty means "nothing in front of us", which
# is the safe default: the bundled proxy replaces the header with the address it
# actually accepted the connection from. Name only the networks you control —
# "10.0.0.0/8" on a plant network is most of the plant.
TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=

# ── Database naming ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cosmetic unless you are pointing this stack at an existing PostgreSQL server.
POSTGRES_USER=plantops
POSTGRES_DB=plantops_iam
PLANTOPS_APP_ROLE=plantops_app

# ── Logging ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# error | warn | log | debug | verbose. `debug` prints the resolved
# configuration at boot with every secret redacted; useful once, noisy after.
LOG_LEVEL=log

# Serve the generated API description at /api/openapi.json. Turn it on when an
# integrating team of yours needs it, off otherwise.
OPENAPI_ENABLED=false

# The `iss` claim in every token this deployment issues, and what a consuming
# module checks. Change it only before the first login: tokens already issued
# name the old value and stop verifying.
JWT_ISSUER=plantops-iam

# ── Session and token lifetimes ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults are the ones the system was designed and tested around. Shortening
# the access-token lifetime does not make revocation faster — revocation is
# immediate and independent of it — it only makes every client refresh more
# often.
#
# 15 minutes.
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=900
# 7 days. This is how long an idle user stays logged in.
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=604800
# 5 minutes, and capped there. A service-account token cannot be revoked before
# it expires, so the lifetime IS the revocation window.
SERVICE_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=300
# 10-30. Two browser tabs refreshing at once must not log the user out; this is
# how long the previous refresh token stays acceptable after being rotated.
REFRESH_REUSE_GRACE_SECONDS=15

# ── Account protection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Consecutive failed logins that lock an account. The lock is administrative: it
# does not lift on its own, and it ends the account's sessions. There is no
# "off" — raise the number if you truly want no lockout.
LOGIN_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS=5
# How long a password-reset link stays usable, in seconds; 24 hours maximum. It
# is a credential sitting in an inbox.
PASSWORD_RESET_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# ── Rate limiting ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The blanket per-caller limit that protects the process. Login and token
# endpoints are tightened separately in code and are not affected by these.
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=120

# ── Caching and background work ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Namespace for every Redis key this installation writes. Change it only if this
# stack shares a Redis with something else, which the bundled one does not.
REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=plantops:
# Safety net behind permission-cache invalidation; 600 seconds maximum. A
# permission change takes effect immediately regardless — this is the bound on
# how long a *missed* invalidation could persist.
GRANTS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=600
# How often expired access grants are swept out of the cache. 0 disables the
# sweep, falling back to the TTL above.
EXPIRY_SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
EXPIRY_SWEEP_BATCH_SIZE=500

# ── Request limits ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Maximum JSON body in bytes. The two document uploads get their own, larger
# ceilings; both must be at least as large as the general one.
REQUEST_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=65536
MANIFEST_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=4194304
BULK_UPLOAD_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES=1048576
# Per-dependency budget for the readiness probe, in milliseconds, so a hung
# database answers 503 quickly instead of never answering.
READINESS_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# ── Key rotation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public keys published alongside the signer, as JSON: { "<kid>": "<pem>" }.
# Leave this alone. It is written for you by the rotation procedure in the
# operations runbook, which enforces an ordering that hand-editing does not.
JWT_RETIRED_PUBLIC_KEYS={}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SET BY THE STACK — documented so a boot failure is readable
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `docker-compose.yml` supplies each of these to the application. They are here
# because the application names them in its error messages, and a variable that
# appears in an error and nowhere in your configuration is the hardest kind to
# chase. Uncommenting one overrides the stack and is almost always a mistake.

# Always `production` in an installed stack.
# NODE_ENV=production
# The port the API listens on *inside* the network. Not the published one; that
# is PLANTOPS_HTTP_PORT above.
# PORT=3000
# The build. Baked into every image and reported by /api/health, which is how
# support answers "what version are you on". Setting it here would let the stack
# claim a version it is not running.
# APP_VERSION=

# The two connection strings, composed from POSTGRES_* and PLANTOPS_APP_* above.
# DATABASE_URL is the application's, carrying the non-owning role; every
# row-level security policy applies to it. DATABASE_DIRECT_URL carries the owner
# and is used only to run migrations.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<app role>:<password>@postgres:5432/<db>
# DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://<owner>:<password>@postgres:5432/<db>
# TLS to the database. `false` for the bundled PostgreSQL, which is reachable
# only on the stack's private network and never leaves the host. Set both of
# these when pointing at your own database server.
# DATABASE_SSL=false
# DATABASE_CA_CERT=
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# `true`, and correct only because the bundled proxy replaces `X-Forwarded-For`
# rather than appending to it. An API exposed directly must have this off.
# TRUST_PROXY=true
# Empty, and correctly empty: the console calls the API at `/api` on its own
# origin, so no cross-origin request is ever made and there is nothing to allow.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# Consumed once and then blanked. The stack hands the migration container the
# value from this file — that migration is the only thing that needs it — and
# hands the application an empty string, so the process serving requests never
# holds a credential it has no use for.
# PLATFORM_BOOTSTRAP_SECRET=
